--TEST--
An enum declaration refuses a `string` backing, a case value that is not an integer literal, PHP's `case` keyword, a member in the body, and an auto-incremented value with no room left in the backing type — `rule:enums/one-backing-type`, `rule:enums/declaration`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:enums/declaring-an-enum

// `rule:enums/one-backing-type`: only `int` and `uint` back an enum, and each
// case's value is an integer literal, so both halves of this declaration are
// refused where they are written.
enum Suit: string { Hearts = "h", Spades = "s" }

// `rule:enums/declaration`: the body is a comma list of bare names, so PHP's
// `case` keyword is refused once per case that writes it.
enum Colour { case Red; case Blue; }

// `rule:enums/declaration`: an enum body holds cases and nothing else, and a
// constant belongs to a class that takes the enum.
enum Size {
    Small,
    Large,
    const int COUNT = 2;
}

// `rule:enums/declaration`: a case with no written value is the previous case's
// value plus one, and nothing is left above the largest `int`.
enum Top { Last = 9223372036854775807, Past }
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0219]: an enum cannot be backed by `string`
%A
error[E0436]: an enum case's value must be an integer literal
%A
error[E0239]: an enum case is not written with `case`
%A
error[E0220]: an enum declares only cases and an optional backing type
%A
error[E0437]: this case's auto-incremented value runs past its enum's backing type
%A
error: aborting due to 7 errors
